show_help_status() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git status  (aliases: st, s)

usage: $US [-V|-D] status [paths...]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS          the toplevel of the repo you are standing in, and every
                 nested repo below it (each one's own .git is read for its
                 status, branch and remote).
  WRITES         NOTHING. 'st' is strictly read-only.

Recursively lists git status of the scoped repos and all their submodules.
Uses  git status --porcelain --ignore-submodules  at each level (submodules
are traversed manually so we can show nested structure).

Default output: ONE LINE PER REPO, ascii-art tree prefix. States:
  CLEAN :))                 — working tree clean, in sync with origin
  CLEAN, ahead N            — clean, local ahead of origin by N commits
  CLEAN, behind N           — clean, local behind origin by N commits
  DIVERGED (ahead X, ..)    — both sides have unique commits
  CLEAN (no remote for B)   — no origin/<branch> tracking ref
  DIRTY (N)  [M:.. ??:..]   — N porcelain entries, grouped by type
  [SKIPPED — cross-user …]  — cross-user policy (warn/ignore) suppressed
  [ERROR …]                 — per-repo failure; doesn't abort the run

The tree includes BOTH submodule-registered and raw (unregistered) nested git
repos found on disk. Marks shown after the display name. The FIRST word says
what the nested git IS relative to its parent — 'registered-sm' (a registered
submodule) vs 'raw-nested-git' (a bare nested .git, not a submodule) — while
'sidecar'/'merged'/'zipped'/'shadowed' are transformed states of such a repo:
  [registered-sm]           — nested git repo registered as a submodule in
                              .gitmodules ('sm' is happy; no action required)
  [raw-nested-git]          — nested git repo NOT registered as a submodule
                              (run '$US sm go' to register it as one)
  [raw-nested-git, ignored] — raw nested repo carrying the GENERIC ignore
                              marker ('.git/my-git.ignore'). NOT sm-specific:
                              my-git leaves it alone everywhere — st does not
                              descend, sm does not
                              register it, mc/flatten skip it. Toggle with
                              'my-git ignore/unignore <path>'; list via
                              'sm --list-ignored'.
  [registered-sm, ignored]  — a registered submodule ALSO carrying that
                              ignore marker — a conflict (it is both
                              registered and marked leave-alone); inspect
                              with 'sm --list-ignored'.
  [STALE REGISTRATION]      — entry in .gitmodules whose on-disk path does
                              NOT exist (phantom registration from a past
                              rename or remove). Run '$US sm --clean-stale'
                              to remove ONLY these — 'sm go' also adds new
                              unregistered repos and rebinds moved ones,
                              which you may not want at the same time.
  [sidecar]                 — directory containing a GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME
                              (config, default '.git.real') — a former
                              registered submodule already converted via
                              'flatten -i' (sidecar mode), or one set up
                              the same way by hand. Plain tracked content
                              as far as git/sm/mc are concerned; nothing
                              to register or flatten. Purely informational.
                              GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME is a self-contained,
                              relocated git history — still a real repo,
                              but the OUTER repo is what tracks ongoing
                              edits now (as plain content), so check the
                              outer repo's status for anything current
                              (see '$US --help flatten').
  [zipped]                  — directory whose git dir has been ARCHIVED by
                              the 'gz' shell helper: it carries a '.git.zip'
                              (zipped plain '.git') or '.git.real.zip'
                              (zipped sidecar) and no live git dir. The
                              worktree files are still here; only the history
                              is currently zipped. Restore with 'gu'. (A live
                              repo that merely keeps a backup zip via 'gz -k'
                              is NOT shown as [zipped].)
  [shadowed]                — content-mirror: the nested repo keeps its LIVE
                              '.git' (so IDE git works) AND the parent ALSO
                              tracks its files. Carries a '.git.shadow.status'
                              marker (first-/last-shadowed timestamps + HEAD/
                              remote). The line still shows the repo's own live
                              CLEAN/DIRTY state. Set up with 'shadow', undo with
                              'unshadow' (see '$US --help shadow').

The top-level scope path carries no tag (it IS the super-repo, not a nested
entry). DIRTY/CLEAN is orthogonal to registration — DIRTY just means
uncommitted file changes, handled by 'mc', not 'sm'.

-V / --verbose promotes to the long form: per-node porcelain lines
indented by submodule depth (the pre-2026 behavior).

Recursion: 'st' is ALWAYS recursive — that is its job (the OVERVIEW
across a multi-repo / submodule tree). -R is accepted silently for
symmetry with 'mc' / 'sm' but has no effect.

Options: -V / -VV / -D / -DD, --help
Scope:   see  $US --help
EOF
}

show_help_masscommits() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git masscommits  (aliases: mc, c)

usage: $US [-V|-D] mc [go] [-i|--interactive] [--sm|--sh] [paths...]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   EVERY repo in scope — this is the one command that writes to
                 many repos in a single run.
  COMMITS TO     each repo's OWN .git, and pushes to each repo's OWN remote.
                 No shared or parent repo receives anything on their behalf.
  SCOPE          single level by default (the named repo(s) only); -R walks
                 every nested repo bottom-up, so a parent's gitlink can bump
                 to a child that was just committed.
  A run prints ' >>> mc: TARGET REPO <paths>' before doing anything.

Modes (two-phase, same pattern as 'sm'):
  default (no 'go')   ANALYZE — one concise line per repo showing current
                      state and the action 'mc go' WOULD take. No writes,
                      no commits, no pushes. Use this first to audit what
                      would happen.
  go                  APPLY — actually add+commit+push. Commit message is
                      produced automatically (never opens \$EDITOR).

Per-repo output in analyze mode (one line each):
  <path>   CLEAN :))                           -> noop
  <path>   CLEAN (no remote for <branch>)      -> noop
  <path>   CLEAN, ahead=<N>                    -> push
  <path>   DIRTY (N) [M:.. A:.. D:.. ??:..]    -> add+commit+push
  <path>   DIRTY (N) [auto:A user:U]           -> auto-commit A + add+commit[+push]
  <path>   DIRTY (N) [auto:A]                  -> auto-commit A

For each repo in scope (go mode):
  1. Audits .claude setup   (CHECK_CLAUDE_SETUP in config; see: --help claude-check)
  2. If dirty:
       Phase 1 (auto) — bumps + recorded paths are committed TRANSPARENTLY
                        via 'git commit --only', no prompt. See 'Auto-commit
                        of my-git-caused changes' below.
       Phase 2 (user) — anything left is user-dirt: git add -A,
                        auto-generate commit message, git commit -F,
                        and 'git push' ONLY if the repo is in GIT_REPOS.
                        Under -i, this phase prompts [Y]es / [S]kip / [A]bort.
  3. If clean  — checks local-vs-remote; pushes if ahead AND pushable; else DONE

Auto-commit of my-git-caused changes (phase 1):
  my-git must never ASK the user to confirm a change my-git itself made.
  Two categories qualify as my-git-caused:
    (a) Submodule gitlink BUMPS — a path in <repo>/.gitmodules whose
        porcelain entry is 'M'. Super-repo recording a new child SHA is
        housekeeping, not a user decision. Under -R bottom-up walk this
        is the common case: children commit, parent's gitlinks advance.
    (b) PATHS explicitly recorded via _mc_record_change during this mc
        run: .gitignore (when ensure_claude_gitignore appends '.claude/')
        and <repo>/.claude (when ensure_claude_setup migrates a real dir
        to a symlink).
  Both are auto-staged and committed together with subject
      my-git mc: auto-commit (bumps=N recorded=M)
  via 'git commit --only -- <paths>', then pushed if pushable. Nothing
  else is committed in phase 1 — user-dirt stays untouched for phase 2.

Push policy:
  A repo is PUSHABLE iff BOTH conditions hold:
    (1) its absolute path matches an entry in GIT_REPOS (the allowlist), AND
    (2) at least one remote is configured ('git remote' non-empty).
  Everything else (submodules walked via -R, third-party forks, repos
  with no configured remote) gets commit only — no 'git push' attempted.
  In analyze output, non-pushable repos show as '-> add+commit' (no push)
  or 'CLEAN, ahead=N  (<reason> → no push)'.
  Reasons: 'not in GIT_REPOS' | 'no remote configured'.

Scope & recursion:
  default             ONE level — act on the current repo only. A changed
                      submodule HEAD is committed as a gitlink bump, but the
                      submodule itself is NOT entered. This matches git's
                      own model: each super-repo is responsible for its own
                      index, not for what lives inside its submodules.
  -R, --recursive     Walk the full submodule tree BOTTOM-UP. Deepest
                      children commit+push first so each parent's gitlink
                      lands on the freshly-pushed child commit in the same
                      run. Use this when you want ONE command to settle a
                      whole tree of nested repos.

Commit-message template (auto-generated, no editor):
  line 1: intro (filename / short file list / 'AUTOMATIC-MASS-COMMIT')
  body:   #-commented block with file list or diffs + last-N commit messages
          (tunables: COMMIT_MSG_LAST_N, COMMIT_MSG_DIFF_MAX_FILES)

Ignore marker (same signal as 'sm'):
  A nested repo carrying '.git/my-git.ignore' is treated
  as "leave alone" — mc silently skips it (counted as ignored=N in
  the summary). Use --force to bypass all markers (matches 'sm --force').

Options:
  go                                 Actually commit+push (otherwise: analyze)
  -n, -T, --try, --dry, --dry-run    Synonyms for analyze mode (never acts)
  -i, --interactive                  Per-repo prompt [Y]es / [S]kip / [A]bort
                                      before each commit+push or push-only
                                      action (shows porcelain + commit subject
                                      for commits; divergence log for pushes).
                                      Also governs destructive .claude migration
  -R, --recursive                    Walk the full submodule tree bottom-up
  --force                            Bypass per-repo my-git.ignore
                                      markers (same as 'sm --force')
  -V, -VV, -D, -DD                   Verbosity / debug
  --help

End-of-run summary:
  mc prints a concise summary (not a full tree) of what was done /
  what's left. For a detailed per-repo status tree, run '$US st'.

Compat:  'my-git go' is a shortcut for 'my-git mc go'.
Scope:   see  $US --help
EOF
}

