show_help_flatten() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git flatten  (alias: fl)

usage: $US [-V|-D] flatten [go] [-i] [--local-only] [--rm-git] [--force] [--sidecar|--merge|--zip [-k]] [path]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the toplevel of the repo you are standing in (it auto-jumps
                 there from a subdir, and says so). Never a nested repo.
  COMMITS TO     that toplevel's .git — the nested files become ITS content.
  ALSO TOUCHES   each nested repo's own .git: --sidecar relocates it,
                 --merge moves its history INTO the toplevel, --zip archives
                 it in place, --rm-git DELETES it.
  A run prints ' >>> flatten: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

Merge every nested git repo under the CURRENT super-repo into the
super's history as regular tracked content. Callable from anywhere
inside the repo (auto-scopes to the toplevel).

THREE modes, differing in what happens to the git dir. --sidecar and --merge
also absorb the nested repo's worktree files into the parent as regular tracked
content; --zip does NOT — it commits ONLY the .git.zip archive and leaves the
worktree files exactly as they were (track them later with 'mc' if you want).
Every item is handled by exactly ONE mode:

  --merge     the git dir stays LIVE in place, or is DELETED. my-git does
              NOT preserve the pre-merge history (only what the parent's
              own commits captured survives; 'unflatten --merge' can
              reconstruct that much, best-effort). The historical default
              for embedded repos and the 'flatten'/'delete' choices for
              registered submodules.
  --sidecar   the git dir is RELOCATED (not deleted) into a self-contained
              GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME capsule beside the files — FULL history
              preserved, usable via 'git --git-dir=<path>/.git.real', just
              no longer IDE/editor auto-discovered. Also writes a parent-
              tracked <path>/GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME.status snapshot (branch,
              commit, remote, core.worktree/bare) so 'unflatten --sidecar'
              restores the EXACT prior state (lossless).
  --zip       the git dir is ARCHIVED into a compact '.git.zip' (plain repo)
              or 'GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME.zip' (an existing sidecar) beside the
              files, then removed; the parent commits ONLY that .zip as content
              (the worktree files are NOT absorbed — unlike --sidecar/--merge)
              ('$US st' shows the dir [zipped]). Lossless — integrity-checked
              ('unzip -t') before anything is removed; tracked worktree
              dotfiles (.gitignore, .gitmodules, …) are left in place.
              -k/--keep also keeps the original git dir. (Same operation as
              the 'gz' shell helper, ported in so no shell function is needed.)

Choosing a mode (all three behave the same way here):
  go --sidecar|--merge|--zip   BULK-apply that ONE mode across scope, no
                               prompting. --sidecar/--merge convert every
                               nested LIVE repo (incl. registered submodules,
                               which bare 'go' leaves untouched); --zip
                               archives every SIDECAR (.git.real -> .zip) AND
                               every plain live nested repo (.git -> .zip)
                               under the toplevel — EXCEPT shadowed repos
                               (skipped in bulk; zip one by naming its path).
                               Pair with --local-only to archive just the ⌂
                               ones.
  go -i --sidecar|--merge|--zip
                               instead of bulk, ask per repo one by one (works
                               with EVERY mode): [Y]es this one / [s]kip it /
                               [a]bort all / [g]o = do this one + all the rest
                               without asking again.
  <path> --sidecar|--merge|--zip
                               restrict to just that ONE repo/sidecar
                               (embedded, registered, orphan gitlink, or an
                               existing sidecar for --zip) — see 'Scope'.

Filter:
  --local-only  (alias --house)  a GENERIC filter over EVERYTHING flatten
                                 does — counts, the by-mode overview, the
                                 plan, and every mode's apply: keep only repos
                                 with NO remote (the ones 'st' marks with the
                                 house icon ⌂). The typical use is
                                 'flatten go --zip --local-only' to archive
                                 only the unpublished sidecars, but it applies
                                 to --sidecar/--merge (and bare flatten) too.

With NO mode flag, bare 'flatten' decides --sidecar vs --merge per item for
the nested live repos it finds (never --zip — that stays opt-in). Each mode
reverses with its 'unflatten' counterpart. See 'my-git --help unflatten'.

Modes:
  default (no 'go')   ANALYZE — print plan (list of nested .git dirs
                      and orphan gitlinks that WOULD be handled). No
                      writes of any kind.
  go                  APPLY — perform the flatten.

A nested repo carrying '.git/my-git.ignore' (same marker
'sm'/'mc' already honor) is left ENTIRELY untouched by default — not
listed as a candidate, not offered in 'go -i'. --force includes it
anyway, same convention as sm/mc's own --force.

Preservation of nested histories (default):
  Each nested .git is briefly MOVED OUT of the outer worktree to a
  temp dir, 'git add -A && git commit' runs on the now-plain content,
  then each .git is MOVED BACK. Net effect:
    - outer's commit contains only the nested content (no gitlinks)
    - each nested repo's history stays intact ('cd nested && git log'
      still works)
    - future edits/adds/deletes inside nested dirs flow through the
      outer repo as normal tracked changes (git's embedded-repo
      detection only fires for NEW untracked dirs; after the first
      flatten commit, the nested paths are already tracked as content,
      so git treats the dir as a plain tracked directory that happens
      to contain a .git inside)
  A trap restores every moved .git on any error / SIGINT / SIGTERM.

  Under 'flatten -i', per embedded repo you can instead choose:
  sidecar    (letter 'r') merge files into parent as content; relocate
             its .git in place to <path>/GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME (config,
             default '.git.real') instead of restoring it as a live
             '.git' — self-contained, addressable via
             'git --git-dir=<path>/.git.real', and never needs the
             move-out/restore dance again for THIS repo, since it's
             no longer named '.git' at all. History fully preserved.
             Set AUTO_SIDECAR_EMBEDDED=1 in config to make bare 'go'
             do this for every embedded nested repo with no prompt,
             instead of the default live-'.git'-kept-in-place
             behavior described above.
  delete     merge files AND delete the nested .git (same as running
             with --rm-git, but just for this one item)
  skip       leave this one nested repo alone entirely

Registered submodules (in .gitmodules) are LEFT UNTOUCHED by bare 'go'
by default — use 'flatten -i' to choose, per submodule:
  skip       leave it alone (default)
  sidecar    (letter 'r') merge files into parent as content; relocate
             its real git directory into <path>/GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME
             (config, default '.git.real') — self-contained and
             portable: moves/copies with <path> as a unit, addressable
             directly via 'git --git-dir=<path>/.git.real', and never
             needs the move-out/restore dance embedded repos require,
             since it's never named '.git' in the first place. Git
             history is fully preserved. Set AUTO_SIDECAR_SUBMODULES=1
             in config to make bare 'go' do this for every registered
             submodule with no prompt.
  flatten    merge files into parent; keep history only as an inert
             backup under the parent's own .git/modules/<name>/ (not
             self-contained — lost if <path> is ever copied/moved on
             its own without the parent's .git alongside it)
  delete     merge files; PERMANENTLY DELETE .git/modules/<name>/ too
A submodule registered but never 'init'ed/cloned (no .git present at
its path at all) instead offers: skip (default) / deregister (drop
the now-meaningless .gitmodules/index entry — nothing to flatten).

After sidecaring: GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME is a self-contained, relocated git
history — a fully real, usable repo (git --git-dir=<path>/GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME
works), NOT a dead archive. What changes is the WORKFLOW: the outer repo
now tracks that content as plain files, so ordinary edits show up in the
OUTER repo's status and are committed there — the sidecar's own object
database isn't advanced automatically as you edit (nothing commits to it
on your behalf). So checking 'git status' via GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME for
day-to-day work isn't useful — it keeps comparing against the commit it
was at when sidecared, and drifts from the worktree the outer repo is
now tracking. Bring it back to the normal live position (its own .git)
any time with 'my-git unflatten --sidecar <path>'.

--rm-git (destructive):
  Instead of move-out-and-restore, each nested .git is DELETED
  permanently. Requires typing literal 'yes' at a mandatory
  confirmation prompt listing every .git that will be deleted. No TTY
  = refusal. Only use when the nested history is genuinely disposable
  (e.g. a tree full of example / learning clones whose outer repo is
  where future work lives).

Why a dedicated operation:
  '.gitignore' with '.git/' or the full relative path does NOT flatten
  nested repos — git's embedded-repo check runs before gitignore rules
  and still stages the nested dir as a gitlink (mode 160000). There is
  no 'git add' flag that treats an embedded repo as content. Renaming
  or deleting the nested .git (what this subcommand automates) is the
  only mechanism that actually works.

Related:
  'my-git sm --unstage-embedded'  — ONLY unstages orphan gitlinks;
                                    doesn't touch the on-disk .git dirs.
                                    flatten does this as a first step.
  'my-git unflatten'              — the reverse: rebuild a live .git,
                                    exactly from a sidecar or, best-
                                    effort, reconstructed from this
                                    repo's own commits for a merged path.

Scope:
  Single-repo only (always). No -R / no walk into submodules. Can be
  called from anywhere inside the repo — auto-scopes to the toplevel,
  same as 'st'. With no PATH argument, acts on the WHOLE repo. With a
  PATH argument, restricts to just that one nested repo (embedded,
  registered submodule, or orphan gitlink) instead. Requires being
  inside a git repo at all.
EOF
}

show_help_unflatten() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git unflatten  (alias: unfl)

usage: $US [-V|-D] unflatten [go] [-i] [--sidecar|--merge|--zip [-k]] [path]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the toplevel of the repo you are standing in.
  COMMITS TO     that toplevel's .git (the teardown commit that drops the
                 now-obsolete sidecar/marker/snapshot artifacts).
  ALSO TOUCHES   each restored path, which gets its OWN live .git back. For a
                 merged path the history comes out of the TOPLEVEL's object
                 database, where 'flatten --merge' put it.
  A run prints ' >>> unflatten: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

Reverse of 'flatten' — rebuilds a live '.git' at a nested path. Three
modes, matching flatten's three:

  --sidecar   EXACT, lossless. <path>/GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME moves back to
              <path>/.git, and core.worktree/core.bare (realize's own
              overrides, only ever correct at the relocated position)
              are unset — a plain <path>/.git needs no override at all.
              Nothing was ever deleted by sidecar mode, so this loses
              nothing. If the path was a registered submodule before
              being sidecared, that's noted on success — re-register it
              yourself with 'my-git sm go' if you want it back in
              .gitmodules (unflatten does not do this automatically).

  --merge     EXACT restore for a path merged in normally: 'flatten
              --merge' transplanted that repo's whole object database
              into this one and pinned its refs, so unflatten fetches
              them straight back — full pre-merge history, original
              branch names and tags. The worktree is left alone (its
              files are usually AHEAD of the merge point), so any
              post-merge drift shows up as ordinary uncommitted changes
              for you to commit.

              BEST-EFFORT RECONSTRUCTION only when there is no preserved
              history to restore — a .git.merged written before
              preservation existed, or whose refs are gone. Then
              the only recoverable history is whatever THIS repo's own
              commits did to that path, extracted via
              'git subtree split --prefix=<path>'. This starts from the
              day it was first merged in, forward — anything before
              that was permanently discarded when the original .git was
              deleted, and is NOT recoverable by this or any other means.
              Bulk-discoverable via its .git.merged marker, written by
              'flatten'/'--merge' just before deletion — removed again
              once unflatten successfully reconstructs a live .git.

  --zip       EXACT, lossless. Un-archives a git dir that 'flatten --zip'
              packed away: '.git.zip' -> '.git', or
              'GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME.zip' -> the sidecar dir. The .zip is
              removed on success (keep it with -k). Refuses to overwrite an
              existing live dir. Bulk-discoverable via its .git*.zip marker.
              Same operation as the 'gu' shell helper, ported into my-git.

Auto-detection: with a PATH and no mode flag, the mode is inferred from
what's actually on disk — a '.git*.zip' means --zip; a GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME
present means --sidecar; otherwise --merge is attempted. Bulk (no path)
processes all three marker kinds unless restricted to one with a flag.

Modes:
  default (no 'go')   ANALYZE — print the plan (which paths, which
                      mechanism). No writes of any kind.
  go                  APPLY (bulk — all discovered items, no prompting).
  go -i               instead of bulk, ask per repo one by one (works with
                      EVERY mode): [Y]es this one / [s]kip it / [a]bort all /
                      [g]o = do this one + all the rest without asking again.

Bulk (no PATH): both modes are discoverable via their own on-disk
marker — sidecars via GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME (same listing 'my-git st' uses
to mark '[sidecar]'), merged paths via '.git.merged' (marks '[merged]'
in 'my-git st').

Related:
  'my-git flatten'  — the forward direction (see 'my-git --help flatten').

Scope:
  Single-repo only (always). Can be called from anywhere inside the
  repo — auto-scopes to the toplevel, same as 'st'/'flatten'.
EOF
}

show_help_shadow() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git shadow   (alias: sh)

usage: $US [-V|-D] shadow [go] [-i] [path]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the toplevel of the repo you are standing in.
  COMMITS TO     that toplevel's .git — it MIRRORS the nested files into it.
  ALSO TOUCHES   nothing in the nested repo's history: its own .git stays LIVE
                 and remains the authoritative history. Shadowing means TWO
                 repos track the same files; the nested one is the real owner.
  A run prints ' >>> shadow: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

The OPPOSITE trade-off from 'flatten'. flatten gets RID of a nested repo's
live '.git'; shadow KEEPS it live — so your IDE/editor git integration
keeps working — while ALSO making the parent repo track the nested repo's
files (a content-mirror / copy). Use it for a repo you both PUBLISH to its
own origin AND actively develop here (README's case 3b): sidecaring it would
break IDE git (nothing recognizes '.git.real'); shadow doesn't.

How (move-aside bootstrap, done once per repo):
  - write a '.git.shadow.status' marker recording first-/last-shadowed
    timestamps + branch/commit/remote (and add it to the nested repo's
    '.git/info/exclude' so it stays out of that repo's OWN status);
  - briefly move <path>/.git OUT of the worktree, so git stops treating the
    dir as a nested-repo boundary;
  - 'git add -f' its files (incl. the marker) into the PARENT index;
  - move .git BACK (live again), and commit the staged content.
  A trap restores .git on interrupt so a crash can't lose it.

The '.git.shadow.status' marker is how 'st' knows to tag the dir [shadowed]
instead of [raw-nested-git]. It's ordinary parent-tracked content.

Why the bootstrap is a one-time thing: git won't 'git add' a file inside a
dir that has its own live .git — it wants to make a gitlink instead. Moving
.git aside for the first add gets a blob tracked under the dir; AFTER that,
git treats the dir as ordinary content and the boundary is transparent —
plain 'git add' / 'git add -A' / 'git status' all descend into it normally.

Afterwards the two stay in sync with NO special mechanism:
  - ordinary 'git add' / 'mc' (git add -A) carry new files + mods + deletions
    straight through — the live .git no longer blocks them;
  - 'last-shadowed' is bumped at COMMIT time by a pre-commit hook that shadow
    installs in the PARENT repo (see below) — 'ga'/'git add' don't touch it.

The pre-commit hook:
  Installed at <parent-git-dir>/hooks/pre-commit as a small managed block
  (delimited by 'my-git shadow (managed block ...)' markers). On every commit
  it bumps 'last-shadowed' to now, and re-stages the marker so it rides in the
  SAME commit, for each shadowed repo that commit stages content into (only
  those — untouched repos are left alone). It is idempotent, and NEVER
  clobbers a pre-existing foreign pre-commit hook (it warns instead, and you
  can paste the managed block in by hand). 'unshadow' removes the block when
  the last shadow under the parent is gone (deleting the hook if it was ours).

Targets:
  <path>       shadow (or re-shadow, refreshing last-shadowed) that one repo.
  (no path)    every EMBEDDED nested live repo NOT already shadowed.
               Registered submodules are NOT shadowed (deregister first).

Modes (same shape as flatten):
  default      ANALYZE — print the plan, change nothing.
  go           APPLY.
  go -i        ask [Y]es/[s]kip/[a]bort/[g]o per repo, one at a time.

Reverse:       $US unshadow   (stop the parent tracking it; .git stays live)
See also:      $US --help flatten
EOF
}

show_help_rehydrate() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git rehydrate

usage: $US [-V|-D] rehydrate [go] [<PATH>]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS          this repo's shadow markers. This repo is NOT modified.
  WRITES         a fresh .git INSIDE each listed project -- nothing else.
                 The files already on disk are never touched.

Shadowing mirrors each project's FILES into the container repo, so a clone
of the container carries all the code. It cannot carry their .git dirs --
git does not track those -- so a fresh clone has every project's source
and cannot push any of it upstream. That is the half a container cannot
deliver on its own.

Each .git.shadow.status records the project's remote and branch precisely
so the other half can be rebuilt. rehydrate clones that history back in
with --no-checkout, so the CONTAINER's copy of the files stays
authoritative; HEAD is pointed at the recorded branch and the index synced
to it, which leaves anything the container holds that upstream does not as
ordinary uncommitted changes for you to review.

  On a new machine:
     git clone <container-url> src && cd src
     $US rehydrate            # what would be restored
     $US rehydrate go         # do it

A project whose marker records NO remote was local-only; there is nothing
to clone. If it was merged rather than shadowed, its history is inside the
container -- see '$US unflatten --merge'.
EOF
}

show_help_diff() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git diff

usage: $US [-V|-D] [--tool PROG] diff [<FILE>]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS          the toplevel of the repo you are standing in, and only its
                 CONFLICTED files.
  WRITES         nothing in the repo. The two versions are written to a
                 fresh temp directory and opened side by side; resolving is
                 still yours to do in the real file.

During a conflict git keeps three versions in the index -- :1 the common
ancestor, :2 OURS, :3 THEIRS -- while the file in your worktree holds
NEITHER: it is the merged text with <<<<<<< markers through it. Reading a
conflict from that file means reconstructing both sides in your head.

This writes the two sides out and opens '$DIFF_TOOL' on them. The temp
names say which side each one is, including the branch:

    my-plex.LOCAL_ours_main.py
    my-plex.REMOTE_theirs_feature.py

The original extension is kept so the viewer still highlights syntax.

With no FILE, every conflicted file is opened in turn ([i of N]).

During a REBASE git's ours/theirs mean the opposite of what most people
expect -- "ours" is the upstream you are replaying onto, "theirs" is your
own commit -- so the labels say 'upstream'/'yourcommit' instead, rather
than a name that means the reverse of what it says.

A side can be ABSENT (add/add has no ancestor; modify/delete has no :2 or
no :3). That side is shown as an empty file and a warning says which.

Options:
  --tool PROG   viewer to use instead of \$DIFF_TOOL (vimdiff by default);
                it receives exactly two paths
  -V, -VV, -D, -DD   verbosity / debug

  \$DIFF_TOOL is set in the config; 'nvim -d', 'opendiff' and 'meld' all work.
EOF
}

show_help_purge() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git purge

usage: $US [-V|-D] purge [go] <PATH> [--from <REPO>] [--force]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the repo whose HISTORY contains <PATH>. Resolved by
                 searching enclosing repos' history, NOT their index -
                 after an 'unshadow' no repo TRACKS the path any more,
                 yet its objects still sit in the repo above, which is
                 exactly when purge is wanted. If more than one repo
                 qualifies, purge refuses and makes you pick with --from.
  REWRITES       every commit in that repo that ever contained <PATH>.
                 EVERY commit SHA changes. A force-push is required.
  DOES NOT TOUCH <PATH>'s own repo, or any other repo.
  A run prints ' >>> purge: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

Removes a path's content AND its entire history from a repo. This is the
counterpart to 'unshadow'/'unflatten', which only answer "who tracks this
path NOW?" - purge answers "who has EVER tracked it?", and is the only
my-git command that rewrites history.

It is its own verb rather than a flag on unshadow on purpose: burying a
history rewrite behind an option on a routine command is how it gets run
by accident. Like '--rm-git' it has NO inverse - in my-git, every
reversible operation comes as a pair (shadow/unshadow, flatten/unflatten,
sm/unsm) and the destructive ones stand alone.

The intended use is reclaiming space AFTER content has moved out: a
nested repo that was merged into its parent, then given its own .git
again, leaves its whole history behind in the parent. purge drops that
dead weight.

SAFETY
  - Refuses unless <PATH> is a repo in its own right (live .git, sidecar
    or zipped), so purge relocates nothing and destroys nothing. --force
    overrides, for the genuine "this really was junk" case.
  - Prints the blast radius first: commits rewritten, MB reclaimed, and
    that every SHA changes.
  - Requires typing 'I am sure' literally. Nothing else proceeds.
  - Copies .git to .git.purge-backup-<timestamp> BEFORE touching anything
    and prints the path at the end. Delete it yourself once satisfied.
  - Uses 'git filter-repo' when installed, otherwise 'git filter-branch'.

  Analyze first (default, read-only):   $US purge src
  Apply:                                $US purge go src --from /syst/global

AFTERWARDS
  Every SHA changed, so publish with a force-push:
     git -C <REPO> push --force --all
     git -C <REPO> push --force --tags
  Any OTHER clone of that repo must be re-cloned or hard-reset.
EOF
}

show_help_unshadow() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git unshadow   (alias: unsh)

usage: $US [-V|-D] unshadow [go] [-i] [path]

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the toplevel of the repo you are standing in.
  COMMITS TO     that toplevel's .git ('git rm --cached' of the mirrored files).
  ALSO TOUCHES   nothing else — the nested repo's own .git is never touched and
                 no history is affected.
  A run prints ' >>> unshadow: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

Reverse of 'shadow'. A shadowed repo has a LIVE '.git', a '.git.shadow.status'
marker, AND its files tracked by the parent; unshadow makes the parent STOP
tracking them ('git rm -r --cached' + commit), deletes the marker, and drops
its '.git/info/exclude' line — so it's a plain nested live repo again. It does
NOT touch '.git' — that was already live (this is the asymmetry with
'unflatten', which REBUILDS a .git). The files stay on disk; the nested repo
keeps working.

Targets:
  <path>       unshadow just that one repo (must be shadowed: a live .git with
               a '.git.shadow.status' marker, or older: parent-tracked files).
  (no path)    every shadowed repo under the toplevel (carries the marker).

Modes (same shape as flatten):
  default      ANALYZE — print the plan, change nothing.
  go           APPLY.
  go -i        ask [Y]es/[s]kip/[a]bort/[g]o per repo, one at a time.

See also:      $US --help shadow
EOF
}

show_help_sync() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git sync  (alias: syn)

usage: $US [-V|-D] sync [go] [-R|-i|--force] [paths...]

Composite "settle the tree" — runs sm → pull → mc in order. Each
phase receives the flags you passed to sync.

  Phase 1: sm    register new nested repos, rebind moved ones, clean
                 stale .gitmodules entries, .claude setup fixes.
  Phase 2: pull  fetch origin everywhere; fast-forward clean+behind.
  Phase 3: mc    auto-commit my-git-caused dirt (gitlink bumps from
                 phases 1+2, .gitignore/.claude fixes), then prompt-
                 gate user-dirt under -i; push where allowed.

Order rationale: sm first so registration is authoritative before
fetch; pull second so we pick up remote changes before committing
local; mc last so any gitlink bumps from earlier phases get captured
in the super's cleanup commit.

Options (all forwarded to each phase verbatim):
  go                 Actually act; otherwise analyze all 3 phases.
  -R, --recursive    Walk full submodule tree (sm top-down, mc bottom-up,
                     pull top-down — each phase handles its own order).
  -i, --interactive  Prompt where applicable in each phase.
  --force            Bypass per-repo my-git.ignore markers.
  -V, -VV, -D, -DD   Verbosity / debug.
  --help

Abort ([A]) in any phase stops the whole sync (exit 77). Earlier-phase
effects already committed stay — my-git's "never undo user commits" rule.
EOF
}

show_help_claude_check() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git claude-check

usage: $US claude-check [--apply] [-i] [paths...]

Audits each repo in scope to enforce the .claude-symlink convention:
  1. .gitignore should contain '.claude/'   -- but ONLY for a repo that
     actually HAS a .claude (see the note below)
  2. <repo>/.claude must be a symlink into \$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR_CENTRAL_REPO
  3. If it is a real dir:
     - settings.json present → HARD ERROR (manual split required)
     - no settings.json      → auto-migrate:
                               mv files → central, rmdir, create symlink

This command is READ-ONLY. It reports one line per repo -- whether it has
a .claude, whether it is published, and anything inconsistent -- and
changes nothing. Enforcement happens in 'my-git mc', which runs the same
checks on a repo it is already committing to.

A repo only ever gets '.claude/' added to its .gitignore if it actually
HAS a .claude. This check used to run unconditionally, so a single
'mc -R' wrote that line into every repo in the tree -- including
third-party clones with no .claude -- and committed it, leaving eleven
repos one unpushable commit ahead of their upstreams.

Options:
  -i, --interactive  Prompt before each migration (in 'mc')
  -V, -VV, -D, -DD   Verbosity / debug
  --help

Config:
  CHECK_CLAUDE_SETUP=0 disables the check entirely in 'mc'.
  CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR_CENTRAL_REPO = $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR_CENTRAL_REPO
EOF
}

show_help_audit() {
  _grd_disp=${GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME:-.git.real}
  cat <<EOF
$US audit [--claude] [--sidecar] [-R] [PATH...]      (read-only)

Read-only health checks over the tree. With no flag, runs BOTH audits.

  --claude     Run the .claude-setup check (same as '$US claude-check').
  --sidecar    Audit the SETUP of every sidecar (.git.real) under scope.

Sidecar audit reports (never changes anything):
  ISSUE  '**/$_grd_disp' missing from the tracked .gitignore — sidecars
         then show up as '??' untracked clutter.
  ISSUE  a sidecar's object-DB directory TRACKED as content in the parent
         index (repo bloat — should be excluded, not committed).
  ISSUE  a NON-portable absolute 'core.worktree' (breaks if moved/cloned;
         portable form is '..').
  ISSUE  sidecar files NOT tracked by the parent — the whole point of a
         sidecar is that its worktree content (INCLUDING the
         '$_grd_disp.status' snapshot) lives in the parent as plain tracked
         files; untracked ones mean the parent isn't capturing it.
  NOTE   an EMPTY sidecar (0 commits) — preserves no history.
  NOTE   a sidecar with no '$_grd_disp.status' snapshot.

Fix the ISSUEs with:   $US repair --sidecar go   (see: --help repair)

PATH...      Limit to the given super-repo(s)/subtree(s); default: all
             configured GIT_REPOS.

See also:      $US --help repair
               $US --help claude-check
EOF
}

show_help_repair() {
  _grd_disp=${GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME:-.git.real}
  cat <<EOF
$US repair [--sidecar] [go] [-R] [PATH...]     (analyze by default)

Fix the SETUP problems that '$US audit --sidecar' reports. --sidecar is the
only target so far, so bare 'repair' analyzes it (it never errors). Analyze-
only unless you add 'go'. Every run ends with a clear one-line SUMMARY, so
you always know whether anything happened.

AUTO-FIXABLE (repair 'go' fixes these; counted, and only then is 'go' offered):
  (A) add '**/$_grd_disp' to the tracked .gitignore and commit it, so
      sidecars stop showing as untracked clutter.
  (B) 'git rm -r --cached' any sidecar object-DB directory that was
      wrongly committed as content, and commit (files stay on disk).
  (C) rewrite an absolute 'core.worktree' to the portable '..'.
  (E) 'git add' + commit any of the sidecar's own worktree files that the
      parent isn't tracking yet.

MANUAL / informational only (repair NEVER changes these — flagged as NOTE,
and 'go' is NOT offered if these are all that's left):
  - EMPTY sidecar (0 commits): 'unflatten --sidecar <path>' to restore an
    empty live repo, or remove its $_grd_disp yourself.
  - no '$_grd_disp.status' snapshot: OPTIONAL ('unflatten --sidecar' works
    without it); not created automatically.

  go           Apply the auto-fixes (default is analyze/dry-run).
  -R           (accepted; sidecar discovery already recurses the subtree.)
  PATH...      Limit to the given super-repo(s)/subtree(s).

See also:      $US --help audit
EOF
}

show_help_submodules() {
  _sm_usage 2>/dev/null || cat <<EOF
my-git submodules  (aliases: sub, sm)

usage: $US sm [opts] [PATH]              (analyze; compact one-line output)
       $US sm [-T|--dry|--try] [PATH]    (preview)
       $US sm go [--force] [PATH]        (apply)
       $US sm --list-ignored             (audit ignored nested repos)
       $US sm --clean-leftovers          (resolve .preexisting.* leftovers)
       $US sm --clean-stale              (remove ONLY stale registrations)
       $US sm --sync-gitignore           (write ignored paths to .git/info/exclude)
       $US sm --emit-gitignore           (print ignore patterns to stdout)
       $US sm --unstage-embedded         (remove mis-staged embedded repos)

WHICH REPO does this touch?
  READS/WRITES   the repo that DIRECTLY ENCLOSES the given PATH — which for a
                 deep path is usually NOT the toplevel you ran from, and NOT
                 the outermost/root repo. With no PATH: the current toplevel.
  COMMITS TO     that same enclosing repo (.gitmodules + index + its .git).
  ALSO TOUCHES   the nested repo's gitdir, which git ABSORBS into the enclosing
                 repo's .git/modules/<name>/. History is moved, never deleted.
  'unsm <path>' de-registers from that same enclosing repo, undoing exactly
  what 'sm <path> go' did.
  A run prints ' >>> sm: TARGET REPO <path>' before doing anything.

Merged from the standalone my-git-submodules.sh tool. Run from the top of a
super-repo to register nested .git trees as proper submodules.

A single positional PATH scopes the run to just that one nested repo
(like 'flatten <path>'), before OR after 'go':
   $US sm go sh/my-git      ==   $US sm sh/my-git go
The submodule is registered into the repo that DIRECTLY ENCLOSES the path
(its immediate parent) — not necessarily the repo you're standing in — so a
deep path registers into the right parent from anywhere, and 'unsm <path>'
always undoes exactly what 'sm <path> go' did.

Sidecar → submodule: 'sm go' also CONVERTS sidecars (GIT_SIDECAR_DIRNAME)
into submodules — it restores the sidecar live (implicit
'unflatten --sidecar') and registers it. A LOCAL-ONLY sidecar (no remote,
shown as ⌂ in 'st') can't be a submodule (git needs a URL), so it's left
as a sidecar. sm is also SIDECAR-AWARE of the enclosing repo: it works
even when the super-repo itself is a sidecar (operates via its .git.real).

Options:
  -D, --debug        set -x plus extra diagnostics
  -V, --verbose      Full multi-line block per candidate
  -i, --interactive  Prompt on failures / missing info
  -R, --recursive    Walk into every registered submodule (top-down)
  --force            With 'go', ignore per-repo ignore markers AND bypass
                     suspicious-URL guards; with embedded paths, reuse a
                     stale .git/modules/<name>/ instead of erroring out
  --help             Show extended help

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Decision table — what 'sm go' does for each situation it encounters.
Every case that can occur is listed here; anything not in this table
is a bug. The table reflects CURRENT behavior, not aspirational design.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  SITUATION                                 DEFAULT                       WITH -i
  -----------------------------------       ---------------------------   ------------------------------
  already registered (path + URL OK)        no-op                         no-op
  nested inside an existing repo boundary   silent skip (see summary)     silent skip (see summary)
   (registered OR not — an unregistered
    enclosing repo's own children are its
    own concern too, not this scope's)
  stale registration                        auto-remove                   prompt [Y]es / [S]kip / [A]bort
   (.gitmodules entry, no dir on disk)
  carries my-git.ignore marker   silent skip (see summary)     silent skip (same)
                                            --force: treat as fresh       --force: treat as fresh
  marker + .gitmodules path mismatch        CONFLICT note, skip           prompt [U]pdate / [R]emove /
   (ignore here, registered elsewhere)                                      [S]kip / [A]bort
  moved (URL matches a different
   registered path), nested repo CLEAN      rebind in place (no re-clone) same (no extra prompt)
  moved, nested repo RISKY                  SKIP with 'push/commit        prompt [P]roceed / [S]kip /
   (uncommitted+untracked, unpushed           first' hint                   [A]bort
    branches, or stashes)
  no origin URL on nested repo              silent skip (counted          prompt for URL
                                             'missing origin URL')
  unregistered with URL, plain new          git submodule add             same
  unregistered with URL, path already
   tracked in index (embedded)              UNTRACK (ls-files -z |        same
                                             update-index -z
                                             --force-remove --stdin) + git submodule add
  URL looks suspicious (unusual scheme)     add anyway (counted)          extra [U]se / [E]dit / [S]kip /
                                                                            [I]gnore perm / [A]bort first
  path matches a .gitignore pattern         silent skip (.gitignore IS    prompt [N]egate / [F]orce /
   (pre-checked with git check-ignore)       the marker — no 'add' run)     [S]kip / [A]bort
                                                                            [N] appends '!<rel>/' to the
                                                                            matching .gitignore, then adds
  stale .git/modules/<name>/                ERROR + skip                  same; --force: reuse + add
   (from an interrupted prior run)
  add fails for an unclassified reason      skip THIS item, continue      [R]etry new URL / [S]kip /
   (unreachable host, repo not found,        with the rest of the plan     [I]gnore perm / [A]bort
    auth failure, timeout, etc.)

Abort ([A]) in any -i prompt exits the whole run with code 77; under -R
it propagates up and terminates the entire recursive walk (not just the
current node).

Auto-commit (go only): at the end of a successful 'sm go', my-git
commits ONLY the paths it touched in this run — .gitmodules, registered
submodule gitlinks, removed stale paths, and any .gitignore files where
a negation was appended via [N]egate. Any unrelated changes the user
had staged or modified (other submodule gitlink drift, unrelated edits)
are LEFT ALONE — they stay staged/unstaged exactly as they were. The
commit message is like 'my-git sm: registered=2 stale-removed=1'. Under
-i, my-git prints the planned message + path list and prompts [Y]es /
[S]kip / [A]bort before committing.

Ignore marker:  writes '<nested>/.git/my-git.ignore' (delete to un-ignore).

Exclude-file sync: at the END of every 'sm go' run (even when nothing was
registered), my-git reconciles \$SUPER/.git/info/exclude so every nested
repo carrying the ignore marker is listed as /path/ there — this prevents
'git status' in the super-repo from showing those repos as Untracked. The
block is delimited by '# --- my-git-submodules: ignored nested repos ...'
markers so unrelated excludes are left alone. --sync-gitignore /
--emit-gitignore still work standalone.

Apply order (top-down): registration is applied parents-first — shortest
path first, deeper paths later. Once a parent has been registered (add or
rebind) in this run, any remaining candidate below it is skipped with a
'now nested inside registered submodule X' note; register those from
INSIDE the parent ('cd X && $US sm go'). This is the OPPOSITE of 'mc',
which is bottom-up so child commits bubble into the parent's gitlink.

Recursion (opt-in): by default 'sm' acts on ONE level — git itself only
cares about the current repo's own gitlinks; what lives inside a
registered submodule is that submodule's own concern. Pass -R /
--recursive to have 'sm' (analyze / --dry / go) walk into every
registered direct submodule top-down, mirroring what 'st' shows, so a
single '$US sm -R go' at the super-repo covers every nested .git in the
tree. [A]bort inside a nested prompt propagates: it aborts the WHOLE
walk, not just the current node. Flag-driven modes (--list-ignored /
--emit-gitignore / --clean-leftovers / --clean-stale / --sync-gitignore /
--unstage-embedded) never recurse even with -R — they already traverse
globally from the current repo.

--clean-stale does ONLY stale-registration removal — nothing else 'sm
go' does (no adding new submodules, no rebinding moved ones, no
ignore-marker handling). Use this when a .gitmodules entry's on-disk
path is gone and that's the ONLY thing you want touched; plain 'sm go'
is not scoped to just that and may trigger unrelated adds/rebinds.
Walks the WHOLE tree regardless of depth (same as --list-ignored) —
checks and cleans EVERY nested repo's own .gitmodules, not just the
current directory's, since a stale entry can live inside an
unregistered nested repo's own registrations just as easily as the
current scope's. Each affected repo gets its own commit, in its own
history.

Stale registrations: entries in .gitmodules whose on-disk path does NOT
exist are detected and (with 'go') removed — 'git config -f .gitmodules
--remove-section submodule.<name>', plus '.git/config' cleanup,
'git update-index --force-remove -- <path>', and 'rm -rf -- .git/modules/<name>'.
Under -i, each stale entry is prompted before removal. Typical cause:
a submodule was renamed on disk without updating .gitmodules, leaving
the old entry behind.

--list-ignored: lists EVERY nested repo that carries the ignore marker.
Entries that are ALSO registered in .gitmodules (same path, or same URL at
a different path) are shown with a '!! CONFLICT' remark — the marker says
"skip" but .gitmodules says "tracked", which is inconsistent state.

Ignored-path mismatch: when 'sm go' detects the conflict (ignored marker
here, but .gitmodules has this URL at a different path), it stops with
status CONFLICT and, under -i, prompts:
   [U]pdate  — rewrite .gitmodules path AND remove the marker, then rebind
               in place (no re-clone; local branches / commits / changes
               preserved).
   [R]emove  — delete the ignore marker only; the next 'sm go' will then
               see this as a normal moved submodule.
   [S]kip / [A]bort.

Rebind auto-save: when 'sm go' rebinds a moved submodule and the nested
repo has ONLY modifications to already-tracked files (no untracked files,
no unpushed commits, no stashes), sm auto-commits them with a message
listing the changed filenames and pushes to origin before re-cloning.
Anything more (untracked files, unpushed commits, stashes) still warns
and skips (or prompts with -i).

See also:      $US --help                (top-level)
EOF
}

