show_help_verbose() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git verbosity: -V / -VV / -D / -DD

Three distinct axes — deliberately separated so a debug run doesn't drag
in -V's (behaviour-changing) medium output:

-V / --verbose        HUMAN medium detail. '>>' info lines + per-repo
                      narration; in 'st' it switches to the per-node
                      format and surfaces the origin remote URL. Does
                      NOT show the raw command trace. This axis can
                      change WHAT a command does/shows, so it's kept off
                      the debug path.
-VV / --very-verbose  The COMMAND TRACE, and ONLY that: every subprocess
                      my-git spawns is echoed as '\$ <cmd>' in dim/grey
                      (on a dedicated fd, so it never contaminates \$(...)
                      captures), just before it runs. Also '>' minor
                      lines. Does NOT enable -V's medium output.
-D / --debug          Implies -VV (command trace) PLUS the '~~' debug
                      lines PLUS the internal "noise" trace (temp-file
                      ops). Does NOT imply -V — so you get commands +
                      diagnostics with -V's behaviour changes staying off.
-DD / --deepdbg       Everything at -D, plus 'set -x' line-by-line shell
                      tracing (and the ' ~ ' deep lines).

Output prefix legend + color conventions (TTY only — colors are
suppressed on non-TTY stdout so logs/captures stay clean):
   >>> MAJOR     bold cyan.   Milestone / headline. First in each block.
    >> medium    blue.         Shown at -V.
     > minor     default.      Shown at -VV (and -D, -DD).
   ~~~ debug     dim (grey).   Shown at -D, -DD. On stderr.
    ~~ debug     dim (grey).   Shown at -D, -DD. On stderr.
     ~ debug     dim (grey).   Shown at -DD only. On stderr.
  \$ cmd        dim (grey).   Command trace — shown at -VV (and -D, -DD),
                              NOT at plain -V. On a dedicated fd (fd 3,
                              dup'd from stderr), so it never contaminates
                              stdout captures (\$(...)).
  ERROR:        red.          stderr. Always shown.
  WARN:         yellow.       stderr. Always shown.
  DONE :))      green inline. Success completion tag inside a headline.
  CLEAN :))     green inline. Repo-state marker.
  DIRTY (..)    yellow inline. Repo-state marker (in st / mc status lines).
  ⌂             local-only marker (no remote to publish to). In st.
  ↑             has-remote marker (a remote is configured — publishable). In st.
  [ABORT ..]    yellow inline. User-triggered abort marker.

The command trace (-VV) and the debug lines (-D) are separate: -D
implies -VV but neither implies -V. -DD adds 'set -x'.
EOF
}

