show_help_push() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git push — publish what is ahead, across the whole tree

usage: $US [-V|-D] push [go] [-i] [--sm|--sh] [--force] [paths...]

Analyzes by default: says which repos are ahead and where they would go.
'go' actually pushes.

Scope is the WHOLE TREE, like 'st' and 'pull' — every live repo at or below
the scope root. Pushing a single repo is what plain git already does.
  --sm / --sub-modules   only the submodule tree (git's own nesting)
  --sh / --shadowed      only the shadowed repos

What it will NOT do:
  * invent an upstream. A branch with no tracking ref is reported, with the
    exact 'git push -u' to run — choosing a remote for you is how work ends
    up in the wrong place.
  * push a diverged branch. It reports 'DIVERGED (n ahead, m behind)' and
    points at '$US pull go', rather than letting git reject it repo by repo.
  * touch local-only repos (the '⌂' rows in st) — there is nowhere to send
    them, which is not an error.
  * touch repos carrying the my-git.ignore marker (--force overrides).
EOF
}

show_help_pp() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git pp — pull, then push, across the whole tree

usage: $US [-V|-D] pp [go] [-i] [--sm|--sh] [paths...]

The tree-wide counterpart of the 'gpp' shell alias (git pull && git push).
Analyzes by default; 'go' applies both halves.

Order matters: a branch that is behind its upstream cannot be pushed, so
pulling first is what turns "rejected, non-fast-forward" into a push that
works. Both halves take the same flags — see '$US --help pull' and
'$US --help push' for what each will and will not do.
EOF
}

